{align:justify|In construction circles today there are three famous sayings:}
{align:justify|Skimp on labor but not on materials: wait for collapse!}
{align:justify|Skimp on materials but not on labor: it will collapse!}
{align:justify|Skimp on both labor and materials: it will certainly collapse!}
{align:justify|In short: "cutting corners and skimping materials" means collapse sooner or later!}
{align:justify|In 1971, in Tainan City, Taiwan, there was a well-known contractor named Truong Si Luong. Unschooled in books, he nevertheless built houses of great sturdiness. There was never any cutting of corners or skimping of materials; he always guarded his reputation and honor. So for private homes and government works alike, everyone wanted him as contractor.}
{align:justify|Truong Si Luong had two sons. The elder, Truong Kim Sung, was a trained design engineer. The younger, Truong Tat Khoan, inherited his father's business.}
{align:justify|At that time the government wanted to build an apartment block in the countryside — a hugely expensive project — and shareholders of both the underworld and the establishment fought to win the contract. Because Truong Si Luong enjoyed a good name, local government officials all recommended him and invited him to take charge of the construction.}
{align:justify|At the county government there was an official named Chiem Tuu Thanh, risen by hard study, who had passed the civil service examination with excellent results. Pity only that he had no self-respect and kept himself unclean. Young in years, he had early learned every evil habit: deceit, bribe-taking, framing the innocent, glib talk masking rotten work, and the like...}
{align:justify|Learning that the eldest son Truong Kim Sung had signed the design contract, he slipped into Kim Sung's house at midnight, bringing along several councilors and triad brothers, falsely claiming the project had been won through many people's influence and muscle — and that now that Mr. Sung had smoothly signed the contract, he must share a bowl of rice with them, what they called an "introduction fee."}
{align:justify|Seeing Mr. Thanh's menacing gang and fearing for his life, Kim Sung had to compromise and agree.}
{align:justify|Next Mr. Thanh led his people to the site manager, the younger son Truong Tat Khoan. Besides demanding a "guarantee fee," Thanh claimed subcontracting rights, naming which construction firms must be used — or else there would be trouble.}
{align:justify|Now this project was never very profitable, and after layer upon layer of threats and extortion,}
{align:justify|Tat Khoan, forced into a cooperation he could not describe, gritted his teeth and let Tuu Thanh take control. And so Tuu Thanh freely manipulated the skimping of labor and materials, cleverly bribing the inspectors to obtain approvals with ease.}
{align:justify|Tuu Thanh believed his methods watertight — no one could ever trace a thing.}
{align:justify|But man proposes and Heaven disposes. The year after the apartment block was completed, Tainan suffered grave floods and typhoons. When the floods passed, the block could not withstand the great storm winds: in a single afternoon the high-rise suddenly collapsed, bricks and tiles flying in all directions.}
{align:justify|And this calamity unfolded precisely as senior officials were touring to survey the disaster — they witnessed it all with their own eyes and ordered their subordinates to investigate.}
{align:justify|The crafty Chiem Tuu Thanh at once dumped all blame on Truong Kim Sung and Truong Tat Khoan. The shrewd Kim Sung, knowing responsibility was hard to dodge, secretly carried money to a central councilor to "handle it fairly," and emerged unscathed.}
{align:justify|Honest Tat Khoan, who knew nothing of bribery, was sentenced to four and a half years in prison. When the news reached Truong Si Luong on his sickbed, finding a lifetime's guarded honor destroyed in a single morning, he could not bear it and took his own life.}
{align:justify|Knowing Tuu Thanh to be cunning and scheming, Truong Kim Sung dared not break off the partnership.}
{align:justify|Tuu Thanh's cousin Chiem Moc Can was also in construction. Whenever Tuu Thanh landed a project he handed it to Chiem Moc Can to build, then leisurely demanded hefty commissions. But Moc Can's projects were never very profitable, and with such high fees to pay, his heart seethed with discontent. Kim Sung saw it and privately invited Moc Can to cooperate: Kim Sung would win the contracts and hand them to Moc Can to build, splitting the gains evenly, demanding no "rose or chrysanthemum fees" at all. Moc Can was delighted and gladly cooperated.}
{align:justify|When word reached Chiem Tuu Thanh, he was furious, feeling Kim Sung and Moc Can had repaid kindness with treachery. That very night he led six triad men to Kim Sung's house to accuse him and teach him a lesson. Little did he know the guards lay in ambush waiting, and the whole gang of villains was caught. In the end, besides being stripped of office, the notorious old fox was sentenced by the court for "instigating attempted murder" to a year and a half in prison.}
{align:justify|When Tuu Thanh had served his time and was released, he joined dark forces to open a construction company, bid for projects, hand them to builders, and skim the profits. Since he controlled countless projects, a great many failures and lawsuits arose. Each time trouble erupted, the police swore to investigate to the end — but after Tuu Thanh's secret dealings with them, the outcome always blurred into approval.}
{align:justify|Though Tuu Thanh slipped through the net of human law again and again, he could not escape Heaven's retribution.}
{align:justify|That day was his 65th birthday. His two sons, so their parents might enjoy a peaceful retirement, had specially pooled funds to build a magnificent villa as a birthday gift for their father — and his birthday was the joyous day of moving into the new house. But no one imagined that that very night the grand villa's foundations would suddenly subside and collapse, burying Mr. and Mrs. Thanh, the eldest son, and the factions that had partnered in the crooked contracts — none could escape, all buried alive beneath the rubble.}
{align:justify|Police investigation later revealed the birthday villa had collapsed from gravely stolen materials. Most astonishing of all, the crew that built it belonged to Mr. Thanh's eldest son's company.}
{align:justify|For Mr. Thanh and his contract-grabbing cliques specialized in pilfering materials, and he had passed this "masterstroke" to his son. His son in turn passed the "secret manual" to the partner builders. And once they had fully mastered it, they unfailingly used the same masterstroke to repay father and son. This is called the retribution of "plant melons, reap melons; plant beans, reap beans."}
{align:justify|In today's society, how many officials and construction companies sell their conscience for money, power, and fame? Look at Taiwan in the great earthquake of '92, and Sichuan and Wenchuan in the great earthquake — how many buildings, hollowed by stolen materials, collapsed and crushed countless unfortunate lives. These are the blood-soaked tragedies born of officials colluding with merchants in conscienceless construction.}
{align:justify|May all who read this true story keep their hearts turned toward goodness. If you use base tricks to win wealth, harming others is still harming yourself, and retribution is inescapable. For ill-gotten money — whether earned by your own hand or your descendants' — can never truly be enjoyed.}
